Per-op status (all six build + link green on the CPU backend; the three that
gained CPU kernels are covered by tests/unittests/test_ggml_fork_ops_cpu.cpp,
which checks each against an independent reference at 1 and 4 threads):
  ggml_graph_set_n_nodes      : trivial shim, fully correct.
  ggml_conv_1d_fast_1d_im2col : composed from ggml_im2col + ggml_mul_mat +
                               reshape -> CPU-functional (matches audio.cpp).
  ggml_flash_attn_ext_with_bias_mask : folds bias into an F16 additive mask
                               and forwards to ggml_flash_attn_ext ->
                               CPU-functional.
  ggml_mul_mat_pack4          : CPU compute dispatches to
                               ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat, matching what the
                               audio.cpp fork and the CUDA backend both do.
  ggml_sage_attn2             : CPU reference kernel (patch 0003). F16 QK^T +
                               softmax + AV in F32, GQA and causal masking
                               matching the CUDA kernel. Skips the INT8/FP8
                               quantization and the K-mean subtraction, both of
                               which are quantization-accuracy devices: mean
                               subtraction shifts every logit for a query by the
                               same constant and so is softmax-invariant.
  ggml_convrot_linear         : CPU reference kernel (patch 0003). Applies the
                               SAME radix-4 orthonormal rotation the CUDA kernel
                               fuses into its activation quantizer -- the
                               weights ship pre-rotated, so the rotation is part
                               of the op's definition, not an optimization --
                               then dot-products against the dequantized INT8
                               weight in F32. Strictly more accurate than the
                               CUDA path (it drops only the lossy activation
                               quantization), which is what a reference wants.

FORK DELTAS AFTER THE CONVERGENCE (patches 0004-0006):
The convergence dropped four audio.cpp fork deltas that the R1 API-drift
inventory could not see, because each is a *behavioural* change inside an
existing kernel, dispatcher or graph builder with no new symbol to grep for.
The MINITTS_* marker grep finds the marked ones cheaply and should still be
re-run against any future fork base -- but 0004 and 0006 were UNMARKED in the
fork, so the grep is necessary, NOT sufficient. The only audits that catch the
unmarked ones are (a) numeric golden gates whose fixtures come from an
implementation-independent reference (flashsr_utility_test's onnxruntime
fixtures caught 0006) and (b) diffing the fork tree against its own upstream
base commit and triaging every hunk:

  0004  two-sided broadcast on binary ops and scale (no marker in the fork; found
        via encoder_module_test aborting on ggml_can_repeat)
  0005  contiguous concat fast paths on CPU (MINITTS_CONCAT_FASTPATH). Perf only,
        but not cosmetic: restoring it is what put
        supertonic_vector_convnext_exp_test, which asserts a >=5% speedup, back
        in the green.
  0006  im2col activation type in ggml_conv_1d / ggml_conv_1d_dw / ggml_conv_2d
        (no marker in the fork; tagged MINITTS_CONV_IM2COL_WEIGHT_TYPE here).
        Upstream forces the im2col'd activations to F16 unless the weight is
        BF16; the fork keeps them in the weight's type, so the framework's F32
        conv weights get F32 convs end to end. Found numerically:
        flashsr_utility_test failed its onnx-golden bound at 1.7e-3 max
        IDENTICALLY on CPU and CUDA -- the signature of a backend-independent
        graph-builder delta -- and passes against the fork tree on the same
        machine/compiler. ggml_conv_2d_dw is deliberately untouched: the fork
        kept upstream's hardcoded F16 there.

The third -- MINITTS_FLASH_PER_HEAD_MASK, the fork's per-head flash-attention
masks on CUDA -- is deliberately NOT restored. Porting it removed the abort but
produced wrong numbers: ggml 0.20.2's CUDA kernels write the per-head index into
the mask pointer (nb32*(head % ne32)) yet still read head 0's slice for every
head. Measured in tests/unittests/test_ggml_fork_ops_cpu.cpp: the CUDA result
matches a reference forced to slice 0 to 4.9e-4 (the F16 floor) while diverging
from the true per-head reference by 9.1e-2. So upstream's blanket
BEST_FATTN_KERNEL_NONE for ne[2] != 1 masks is load-bearing, not conservatism,
and the choice belongs one level up: the relative-attention module now keeps
that lowering on CPU and falls through to the reference path elsewhere
(src/framework/modules/attention/common_relative_attention.cpp). Restoring the
fork delta here would trade a loud abort for a silent numerical error.

Patch 0004 was applied in place by commit e11e3c5 and was NOT tracked as a
patch until now -- the next sync-ggml.sh run would have deleted it silently.
That is the failure mode this manifest warns about, and it happened; anything
added to this tree must land under patches/ggml/ in the same change.

REPRODUCIBILITY: verified 2026-08-20 on Windows/Git-Bash. `scripts/sync-ggml.sh`
with patches 0001-0005 regenerates this tree with no content difference at all:
`git diff --ignore-cr-at-eol -- external/ggml` is empty, and UPSTREAM itself is
byte-identical.

A plain `git diff` after a sync does show ~13 paths on Windows. That is line
endings only. The script extracts via `git archive | tar`, which writes LF, while
many blobs in this tree are committed with CRLF (1145 of 2163 -- see the mixed
line-endings note in the changelog), so the on-disk bytes differ where the
content does not. Check with --ignore-cr-at-eol before concluding a sync changed
anything, and do not "fix" it by renormalizing: that is a 1145-file sweep whose
only effect here is churn.

